.TH SDE-APPIMAGE 1 "October 2026" "SDE" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sde-appimage \- run an AppImage after verifying its signature
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sde-appimage
\fIFILE\fR [\fIARGUMENTS\fR...]
.br
.B sde-appimage
\fB--check\fR \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B sde-appimage
\fB--forget\fR \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B sde-appimage
\fB--version\fR | \fB--help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBsde-appimage\fR (the \fBAppImage Runner\fR of the Selviana Desktop
Environment) runs AppImages with a double-click: Liria and the desktop open
AppImages with it, also those that are not marked executable, so they do
not need \fBchmod +x\fR. It is not shown in the Start menu.
.PP
Before an AppImage runs for the first time, sde-appimage checks that it is
an ELF program for this computer with the AppImage magic bytes (type 1 or
2) and verifies the OpenPGP signature that \fBappimagetool --sign\fR embeds
in type 2 AppImages (the \fI.sha256_sig\fR section, made over the SHA-256
digest of the file, with the signer's public key in \fI.sig_key\fR) with
\fBgpgv\fR(1) (or \fBsqv\fR(1)), trusting no key but the one in the file.
.PP
A valid signature proves that the file is unchanged since it was signed; it
does not prove who signed it. The key that signed the first version of an
application that was opened is therefore remembered (trust on first use),
and later versions of the same application (the same update information,
or the same file name without version and architecture) must be signed
with the same key.
.PP
An AppImage that passes runs at once: it becomes executable for its owner
and the verified open file is executed, so no other file can take its place
in between. Its arguments are passed on and its exit status is
sde-appimage's. Without FUSE (\fI/dev/fuse\fR, \fBfusermount\fR and, for
older AppImages, \fIlibfuse.so.2\fR) it runs with
\fBAPPIMAGE_EXTRACT_AND_RUN=1\fR. An AppImage on a drive mounted
\fBnoexec\fR, or one that other users may change, runs from a private copy
in \fI~/.local/share/sde/appimages\fR that is verified again.
.PP
Any other AppImage does not run. A window explains why (no signature, a
signature that does not match, a signature without its key, another key
than earlier versions, an old type 1 AppImage, another architecture, not
an AppImage at all) and offers \fBDelete\fR (the default: moves it to the
trash) and \fBKeep\fR (leaves it where it is and takes its executable bits,
so it does not run by accident). Without a display the explanation is
printed and nothing runs.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --check " " \fIFILE\fR
Prints the verdict (\fBtrusted\fR, \fBunsigned\fR, \fBbad-signature\fR,
\fBno-key\fR, \fBkey-changed\fR, \fBtype1\fR, \fBwrong-arch\fR,
\fBnot-appimage\fR or \fBunreadable\fR), the digest, the application, the
key and its user id, and exits 0 when the AppImage would run. A valid
signature of a new application is remembered as when it is opened.
.TP
.BR --forget " " \fIFILE\fR
Forgets that \fIFILE\fR was verified or accepted, its private copies and
the key of its application: the next version of the application is trusted
on first use again (for an application whose maker announced a new key).
.SH POLICY
The administrator chooses in \fI/etc/sde/appimage.conf\fR:
.TP
.B policy=verified-only
Only AppImages with a valid signature run (the default).
.TP
.B policy=ask
AppImages without a signature, without their key or of type 1 may also run
after the warning, with \fBRun Anyway\fR; the contents are remembered and
run again without asking. A signature that does not match and a changed
key never run.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sde/appimage.conf
The policy.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sde/appimage/trust.tsv
What was verified or accepted (SHA-256 digests), the publisher keys and the
files already checked (device, inode, size and change time, so an unchanged
file is not read again).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sde/appimages/
Private copies of AppImages that cannot run where they are.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/sde/appimage.log
What was run, accepted, refused, kept or deleted.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
\fBSDE_APPIMAGE_POLICY\fR replaces the policy file, \fBSDE_APPIMAGE_TRUST\fR
the trust store and \fBSDE_APPIMAGE_GPGV\fR the verifier (tests; "-": none). Without it \fI/usr/bin/gpgv\fR is used, else \fBsqv\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The application's exit status when it ran; 1 when it did not run (kept,
deleted, refused or an error); 2 for a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBgpgv\fR(1), \fBdebrun\fR(1), \fI/usr/share/doc/sde/APPIMAGE.md\fR
